# Civics, law and public policy, stages 0 to 5.
# Sort claims from evidence, weigh readings of a rule, and build a reasoned case.
# Towns, rules and cases are invented. This is not legal advice.

@ civics.0.taking-turns
goal: Explain why we take turns, and know to ask a grown-up when a rule feels unsafe or unfair.
intro: A rule tells everyone what to do when we share something. "Take turns" is a rule. It gives every child a go. Good rules have a reason.
  show: emoji 🛝 👧 👦
demo: Two children want one swing. A grown-up helps them to agree on short turns. Each child tells the next when the turn is over. Now nobody has to guess, and waiting is easier.
  show: flow wait > have a turn > tell the next child > swap
P: Why do we go down the slide one at a time?
  + So that everyone has a go, and there is room to land safely :: The rule keeps it fair and safe.
  - Because slides are boring :: [no-reason] The rule has a real reason: fairness and safety.
  - So that the biggest child goes first :: [biggest-first] Turns are for everyone, not for the biggest.
  hint: What could happen if two children went down together?
  why: Taking turns gives each child a go and keeps everyone safe.
O: Put the steps for taking turns on the swing in order.
  items: Wait in the line | Have your turn | Get off when your turn ends | The next child has a turn
  ends: First | Last
  hint: You wait before your turn.
  why: Wait, swing, stop, and then it is the next child's turn.
G: A fair rule, or not a fair rule?
  bin A fair rule: One at a time on the slide | Wait for your turn | Keep your hands to yourself
  bin Not a fair rule: Only tall children may play | The new child never has a turn
  hint: Does the rule give everyone a chance?
  why: A fair rule is the same for everyone, and has a good reason.
>P: A bigger child says, "Give me your coat, or you cannot play." Is that a rule you have to follow?
  + No: tell a grown-up you trust :: It is unfair, and it does not feel safe.
  - Yes: you must always do what you are told :: [obey-everything] You do not have to obey something that feels unsafe or unfair.
  - Yes: bigger children make the rules :: [biggest-decides] Being bigger does not make it a rule.
  hint: Does it feel fair and safe?
  why: Following a good rule is different from doing everything that anyone tells you. If something feels wrong, ask a grown-up for help.
done: Good rules are fair and have a reason. Find one rule at home and say why it is there.

@ civics.0.community
goal: Name places that we all share, know who looks after them, and tell a grown-up when something is broken.
intro: A community is the people who share a place. Parks, paths and libraries belong to everyone. Many helpers look after them. We can help too, in small and safe ways.
  show: emoji 🌳 📚 🚮
demo: A bench in the park is broken, and there are sharp bits. A child tells a grown-up. The grown-up tells the people who mend benches. The child finds another seat, and does not try to mend the sharp bits.
  show: flow see a problem > tell a grown-up > the helpers mend it
G: A place we all share, or something that is just mine?
  bin We all share it: the park | the library | the path
  bin Just mine: my toothbrush | my bed | my coat
  hint: Can everybody use it?
  why: Parks, libraries and paths are for everyone. That is why we all look after them.
M: Match each helper to the job.
  pairs: Librarian => Helps you to find a book | Park keeper => Looks after the park | Street cleaner => Keeps the street clean | Plumber => Mends the water pipes
  hint: Who works among the books?
  why: Many people do jobs that keep our shared places working.
P: You see a broken swing with a sharp edge. What should you do?
  + Tell a grown-up you trust :: They can tell the people who mend it.
  - Mend it yourself :: [unsafe-choice] The sharp edge could hurt you.
  - Keep on playing on it :: [unsafe-choice] A broken swing is not safe.
  hint: What did the child do about the broken bench?
  why: Telling a grown-up is the safe way to help. Mending is a job for the right helpers.
>P: You see an empty packet on the path in the park. What is a safe way to help?
  + Ask a grown-up to help you put it in the bin :: You are caring for a place that everyone shares.
  - Drop your own litter too :: [made-it-worse] That makes the park worse for everyone.
  - Pick up some broken glass as well, by yourself :: [unsafe-choice] Broken glass is for a grown-up to deal with.
  hint: Who can help you to do it safely?
  why: Everyone can help to look after shared places, in ways that are safe for them.
done: Shared places belong to everyone. Notice one helper at work this week.

@ civics.1.fair-decisions
goal: Count a vote, tell voting from agreeing together, and check that a decision leaves nobody out.
intro: When people in a group want different things, the group needs a fair way to choose. In a vote, each person has one say, and the choice with the most votes wins. In a consensus, people keep talking until they find something that everyone can accept.
  show: table Way of choosing | How it works ;; voting | count the hands: the most wins ;; consensus | talk until everyone can accept
demo: Six children choose a game. Four want a climbing game. Two want to draw. Before they vote, they find out that one child has a sore arm and cannot climb. So they look for a game that all six can play.
  show: bars Climbing=4 Drawing=2 unit=votes
F: Count the vote.
  frame: Tag has 5 votes and the puzzle has 3 votes. The winner of the vote is __
  taps: tag | the puzzle
  answer: tag
  x the puzzle :: [chose-the-smaller] Three is fewer than five.
  hint: Which number is bigger?
  why: 5 is more than 3, so tag has the most votes.
M: Match each word to how it works.
  pairs: Voting => Count the hands; the most wins | Consensus => Keep talking until everyone can accept the choice | Discussion => Say what you need, and listen to others
  hint: Which one counts hands?
  why: Voting is quick. Consensus takes longer, but everybody can live with the result.
P: What should happen before a vote?
  + Everyone hears the choices and has a chance to speak :: Then people know what they are choosing.
  - The loudest child chooses :: [loudest-wins] A vote gives each person the same say.
  - Nothing: just vote :: [skipped-discussion] People need to know the choices first.
  hint: How can you choose if you do not know the choices?
  why: A fair vote begins with clear choices and a chance for everyone to be heard.
>P: The class votes 20 to 5 to hold its party on Friday. Five children have a music lesson every Friday and cannot come. What should the class do?
  + Look for a day when everyone can come :: Winning the vote does not make it fair to leave them out.
  - Keep Friday: the vote was won :: [majority-is-always-fair] A vote that shuts some children out is not a fair result.
  - Cancel the party :: [gave-up] There may be a day that suits everyone.
  hint: Could those five children come on another day?
  why: A good decision counts everyone's needs, including those who were outvoted.
done: Count fairly, and leave nobody out. Help your family choose something by listening to everyone first.

@ civics.1.rights-responsibilities
goal: Explain that everyone has rights simply by being a person, and connect rights with how we treat one another.
intro: Every person matters. That is called dignity. Because every person matters, everyone has rights: to be safe, to be listened to, to learn. A right is not a prize. You do not have to earn it.
  show: table A right | A reward ;; for everyone | for something you did ;; cannot be taken away for being naughty | can be earned or missed
demo: Two children have the same chance to hear a story. One of them does not hear well, and needs to sit nearer the front. Giving her the nearer seat helps her to join in. It does not mean that she matters more than anyone else.
  show: flow the same story for all > one child needs to sit nearer > everyone can hear
G: A right for everyone, or a reward that you earn?
  bin A right: to be safe | to be listened to | to learn
  bin A reward: a sticker for tidying up | a prize for winning a race | a star for good spelling
  hint: Does every child have it, whatever happens?
  why: Rights belong to everyone. Rewards are given for something you have done.
P: Sam was unkind today, and lost his sticker. Does Sam still have the right to be safe?
  + Yes: rights are not prizes :: Sam lost a reward, not a right.
  - No: he was unkind :: [rights-as-rewards] A right cannot be lost by behaving badly.
  hint: Is being safe a prize?
  why: Everyone keeps their rights, even on a bad day.
M: Match each right to what you owe to others.
  pairs: I may speak => I listen when others speak | I may be safe => I keep my hands to myself | I may use the books => I look after the books | I may play => I let others join in
  hint: If you want to be listened to, what should you do for others?
  why: My rights and other people's rights fit together. Looking after theirs is my responsibility.
>P: A child who uses a wheelchair goes up in the lift. The other children use the stairs. Is that child being treated as more important?
  + No: the lift gives the same chance to get upstairs :: Different help, the same chance.
  - Yes: the child gets something special :: [same-treatment-is-fairness] Without the lift, the child could not get upstairs at all.
  - Yes: everyone should use the stairs :: [same-treatment-is-fairness] Then one child would be left downstairs.
  hint: Where would the child be without the lift?
  why: People can need different support in order to have the same chance.
done: Rights are for everyone, and we look after one another's. What is one thing you can do so that someone else is heard?

@ civics.2.institutions
goal: Explain how public decisions are made, carried out and reviewed, and why those jobs are kept apart.
intro: An institution is a lasting arrangement of roles and rules, such as a council, a court or a school board. Many systems separate three jobs: making the rules, carrying them out, and reviewing disputes about them. Each can then check the others.
  show: flow make the rule > carry it out > review a dispute
demo: In an invented town, the council sets the opening hours of the park. The park staff open and lock the gates. An independent panel hears complaints that the rule was applied unfairly. If one person secretly controlled all three, nobody could challenge a bad decision.
  show: table Job | Who does it ;; makes the rule | the council ;; carries it out | the park staff ;; reviews complaints | an independent panel
G: Who is doing what?
  bin Making a rule: The council votes on the park's opening hours. | The school board sets a policy on uniform.
  bin Carrying it out: Park staff open and lock the gates. | Teachers apply the uniform policy.
  bin Reviewing a dispute: An independent panel hears a complaint of unfair treatment. | A court decides whether a rule was lawful.
  hint: Is it deciding the rule, applying it, or checking how it was applied?
  why: Making, carrying out and reviewing are different jobs. Keeping them apart lets each be questioned.
O: Follow a decision from proposal to review.
  items: The council proposes a rule | The public can comment on it | The council votes | The staff carry it out | A resident complains that it was applied unfairly | An independent panel reviews the complaint
  ends: First | Last
  hint: A rule has to be made before it can be applied, and applied before anyone can complain.
  why: Proposal, comment, decision, action, complaint, review. Each stage is a chance to catch a mistake.
M: Match each safeguard to what it does.
  pairs: Written rules => Everyone can find out what the rule is | Public reasons => People can see why a decision was made | A right of appeal => A decision can be challenged | Independent review => Someone with no stake checks it | Elections => Those who make the rules can be replaced
  hint: Which one lets you see the "why"?
  why: Each safeguard makes authority answerable in a different way.
P: Why does it matter that the panel reviewing complaints is independent of the council?
  + A body cannot be trusted to judge complaints about itself :: It has a stake in being found right.
  - It does not matter: the council knows the rule best :: [self-review] Knowing the rule is not the same as judging fairly whether it was misapplied.
  - Because panels are cheaper than councils :: [irrelevant-reason] The point is impartiality, not cost.
  hint: Would you want the other team's captain to referee your match?
  why: A review means something only when the reviewer has no interest in the outcome.
>P: In a club, one person collects the money, keeps the accounts and also checks them at the end of the year. What is the risk, and the remedy?
  + Nobody else would notice a mistake or a misuse: a different person should check the accounts :: Separate the roles.
  - There is no risk if that person is honest :: [trust-in-place-of-checks] Honest people make mistakes too, and checks protect them as well.
  - The club should stop collecting money :: [overcorrection] The remedy is a check, not giving up the activity.
  hint: Who would notice an error?
  why: When one person holds every role, nothing stands between an error and its consequences. Separation creates a check.
done: Make, carry out, review, and keep them apart. Find out who reviews decisions at your school or club.

@ civics.2.evidence-hearings
goal: Separate what was observed from what was inferred, weigh the limits of a source and explain what makes a hearing fair.
intro: A claim says what someone believes happened. Evidence gives reasons to accept or to doubt it. An observation reports what was seen or recorded. An inference is a conclusion drawn from it. Fair decisions keep the two apart.
  show: table Observation | Inference ;; "I saw Kit beside the table." | "Kit must have knocked it over." ;; what was seen | what someone concluded
demo: A class model is found on the floor. One pupil saw Kit standing near the table. But being near the table does not show that Kit touched the model. A photograph with the time on it, and each pupil's own account, may fill in more of the story, and may still leave gaps.
  show: flow someone was near > did they touch it? > look for more evidence
G: Observation or inference?
  bin Observation: I saw Kit beside the table at five past ten. | The photograph shows the model on the shelf at ten o'clock. | The model was on the floor at ten past ten.
  bin Inference: Kit must have knocked it over. | Kit looked guilty. | Nobody else could have done it.
  hint: Could a camera have recorded it?
  why: Observations report what was perceived. Inferences go beyond that, and they can be wrong.
K: Who knocked the class model off its shelf? Turn over as few pieces of evidence as you need to decide whether Kit did it.
  clue Witness: A pupil saw Kit standing near the table.
  clue Photograph: A photograph with the time on it shows the model already on the floor before Kit came into the room.
  clue Last week: Kit was told off last week for something else.
  clue Impression: Another pupil says that Kit "looked guilty".
  need: 2
  + Kit did not do it: the model was already on the floor :: The photograph settles it.
  - Kit did it: Kit was near the table :: [nearness-as-proof] Being near does not show that Kit touched it.
  - Kit did it: Kit has been in trouble before :: [past-behaviour-as-proof] What happened last week is not evidence about today.
  hint: Which piece of evidence shows the order in which things happened?
  why: A record of when things happened can rule a person out. Nearness, reputation and impressions cannot rule anyone in.
S: Select every feature of a fair hearing.
  + Each person may give their account :: Both sides are heard.
  + The person deciding has no stake in the result :: Impartiality.
  + The evidence is examined before the decision :: Decide on the evidence.
  + The reasons for the decision are explained :: People can see how it was reached.
  - The most confident speaker is believed :: [confidence-as-truth] Confidence does not show that someone is right.
  - The first person accused is assumed to have done it :: [presumed-guilt] An accusation is a claim to be tested.
  hint: What would you want if you were the one accused?
  why: A fair hearing hears everyone, is decided by someone impartial, rests on evidence and gives reasons.
P: A witness says that she is absolutely certain what she saw. She was thirty metres away, and it was getting dark. How should her account be treated?
  + As useful but limited: being certain does not make up for a poor view :: Weigh how well she could see.
  - As decisive: she is certain :: [confidence-as-truth] People can be certain and mistaken.
  - As worthless :: [overcorrection] It is still evidence. It simply carries less weight.
  hint: What affects how well a person can see?
  why: A source's value depends on its opportunity to observe, not on how sure it sounds.
>P: Two pupils disagree about who broke a window. The teacher who is to decide is the aunt of one of them. What is the problem?
  + She has a personal connection, so she is not impartial: someone else should decide :: Even a fair decision would not look fair.
  - There is no problem if she tries to be fair :: [good-intentions-suffice] A hearing must be seen to be fair, as well as be fair.
  - The nephew should win automatically :: [bias-accepted] That is exactly the unfairness to be avoided.
  hint: Would the other pupil trust the result?
  why: A decision-maker should have no personal stake. Where there is one, another person should decide.
done: What was seen, what was concluded, and who is deciding? Listen for an inference presented as a fact today.

@ civics.3.legal-reasoning
goal: Analyse a rule by issue, rule, application and conclusion; compare a reading by the words with a reading by the purpose; and test an analogy.
intro: Legal reasoning links a question to a rule, and the rule to the facts. The words of a rule give its text. Its purpose is what it aims to achieve. Earlier decisions may guide later ones. Good analysis states where the rule is uncertain, and does not pretend that the wording settles everything.
  show: flow issue > rule > application > conclusion
demo: A library has the rule "No animals in the library", made to keep it quiet and clean. A visitor arrives with a guide dog. By the words, a dog is an animal. By the purpose, a trained guide dog threatens neither quiet nor cleanliness, and to exclude it would exclude its owner. The rule does not settle the case by its wording alone.
  show: table Way of reading | It asks | The guide dog ;; by the text | what do the words say? | it is an animal ;; by the purpose | what is the rule for? | it threatens neither aim
O: Put the four steps of a legal analysis in order.
  items: Issue: state the question | Rule: state the rule that applies | Application: apply the rule to the facts | Conclusion: answer the question
  ends: First | Last
  hint: You need the question before the rule, and the rule before you apply it.
  why: Issue, rule, application, conclusion. The order makes the reasoning easy to follow, and to challenge.
C: Two readings of "No animals in the library", applied to a guide dog. How do they differ?
  left: text The rule says "animals". A guide dog is an animal. So it is excluded.
  right: text The rule exists to keep the library quiet and clean. A guide dog threatens neither, and excluding it would bar its owner.
  + The left reads the words and the right reads the purpose; a sound analysis sets out both, and the doubt between them :: Two legitimate methods.
  - The left is the only correct method :: [text-only] The wording does not show whether the rule was meant to reach this case.
  - The right simply ignores the rule :: [purpose-as-evasion] It interprets the rule by what the rule is for.
  hint: What does each reading treat as decisive?
  why: Text and purpose are both sources of meaning. Where they pull apart, the honest answer names the ambiguity.
M: Match each source of legal reasoning to what it supplies.
  pairs: Text => The ordinary meaning of the words | Purpose => What the rule is meant to achieve | Precedent => How an earlier, similar case was decided | Analogy => A comparison with a case already settled
  hint: Precedent looks back to earlier decisions.
  why: Different systems weigh these sources differently, and all four appear in reasoned argument.
P: Last year a pet cat was turned away under the same rule. Is the guide dog's case like the cat's?
  + Alike on the surface, since both are animals, but different in what matters: the dog is an aid on which its owner depends :: The difference bears on the rule's purpose.
  - Exactly alike: both are animals :: [surface-analogy] A shared label is not a relevant likeness.
  - Entirely unlike: a dog is not a cat :: [surface-distinction] The species is not the difference that matters.
  hint: Which difference between the two cases bears on why the rule exists?
  why: An analogy holds only when the cases are alike in the respects that matter to the rule.
B: Assemble an analysis of the guide-dog case in order.
  bank: Issue: does "No animals in the library" exclude a guide dog? | Rule: animals are barred, in order to keep the library quiet and clean. | Application: a guide dog is an animal in the ordinary sense, but threatens neither aim, and excluding it bars its owner. | Conclusion: read by its purpose, the rule admits the dog; the wording leaves real doubt. | Dogs are nicer than cats. | The librarian does not like dogs.
  answer: Issue: does "No animals in the library" exclude a guide dog? | Rule: animals are barred, in order to keep the library quiet and clean. | Application: a guide dog is an animal in the ordinary sense, but threatens neither aim, and excluding it bars its owner. | Conclusion: read by its purpose, the rule admits the dog; the wording leaves real doubt.
  x Dogs are nicer than cats. :: [irrelevant-preference] A personal preference is not a reason in law.
  x The librarian does not like dogs. :: [irrelevant-preference] The decision-maker's taste is not part of the rule.
  hint: Question, rule, facts set against the rule, and then the answer.
  why: A structured analysis shows each step, so that a reader can see exactly where to agree or disagree.
>P: A sign reads "No vehicles in the playground". It was put up after a child was struck by a car. Does it ban a pushchair?
  + By the words, perhaps; by the purpose, no: state the doubt, and prefer the reading that fits the purpose :: A pushchair has wheels and carries someone, but it is not what the sign was put up against.
  - Yes, plainly: a pushchair is a vehicle :: [text-only] That reading would defeat the rule's own purpose of protecting children.
  - No, plainly: the sign means cars :: [purpose-only] The wording is wider than "cars". The doubt is real and should be acknowledged.
  hint: Why was the sign put up?
  why: Sound interpretation presents the strongest form of each reading, and explains its choice between them.
done: Issue, rule, application, conclusion, and an honest statement of doubt. Analyse one rule from a place you know.

@ civics.3.participation
goal: Map the people affected by a public decision, recognise what keeps some of them from taking part, and separate questions of evidence from questions of value.
intro: A public decision affects different people differently. Good analysis starts by listing who is affected and how. It then asks who has actually been heard, and it keeps two kinds of question apart: what will happen, and what matters most.
  show: table Kind of question | Example | Settled by ;; evidence | will traffic fall? | measurement ;; value | does quiet matter more than speed? | public reasoning
demo: An invented town is considering closing a shopping street to cars. Shopkeepers, wheelchair users, delivery drivers, pedestrians and the residents of neighbouring streets would each be affected differently. An online survey gathers 400 replies, but it misses those without internet access.
  show: chips shopkeepers | wheelchair users | delivery drivers | pedestrians | neighbouring residents
M: Match each group to its likely concern.
  pairs: Shopkeepers => Whether customers can still reach them | Wheelchair users => Whether there is a drop-off point close by | Delivery drivers => Where they may stop to unload | Pedestrians => A safer and quieter street | Residents of neighbouring streets => Traffic moving onto their roads
  hint: Who gains a quieter street, and who inherits the traffic?
  why: Mapping those affected shows that one policy has many different effects.
G: A question of evidence, or a question of value?
  bin Evidence: Traffic fell by 30% during the trial. | Deliveries took ten minutes longer on average.
  bin Value: Quiet streets matter more than quick deliveries. | Access for disabled residents should come first.
  hint: Could it be measured, or does it state a priority?
  why: Evidence tells us what happens. Values tell us what we care about. Policy needs both, and should not mistake one for the other.
P: The town's only consultation was an online survey. What is the weakness?
  + It leaves out people without internet access, who may be affected differently :: Those who are missing are not random.
  - There is none: 400 replies are plenty :: [size-over-coverage] The number of replies does not cure the absence of whole groups.
  - Online surveys are always worthless :: [overcorrection] They are useful as one route among several.
  hint: Who cannot answer an online survey?
  why: A consultation is only as representative as its means of reaching people. Several channels reduce the gaps.
P: A councillor says, "The evidence shows that the street should be closed." What is wrong with that sentence?
  + Evidence shows the effects; whether those effects justify closing the street is a choice of values :: The two have been run together.
  - Nothing: evidence decides policy :: [evidence-settles-values] Evidence informs the choice. It cannot make it.
  - Evidence has nothing to do with policy :: [values-only] The effects matter. They are simply not the whole of the decision.
  hint: Does evidence alone say what "should" be done?
  why: A "should" needs a value as well as a fact. Stating the value openly lets others agree or disagree with it.
>P: A consultation meeting is held at two o'clock on a weekday, up a flight of stairs, with a ninety-page technical report. Who is likely to be missing, and what would help?
  + People at work, carers, people who cannot manage stairs and non-specialists: offer several times, accessible places and a plain summary :: Remove the barriers.
  - Nobody: the meeting is open to all :: [formal-openness] It is open in name, and closed in practice to many.
  - Only people who do not care :: [blamed-the-absent] Absence may reflect barriers, not indifference.
  hint: Who can come at two on a weekday, climb stairs and read ninety pages?
  why: Participation is unequal when taking part costs some people far more than others. Lowering those costs widens who is heard.
done: Who is affected, who was heard, and is it a fact or a value? Map those affected by one local decision.

@ civics.4.comparative
goal: Compare legal and constitutional institutions by what they do in practice, and limit the causal claims drawn from a handful of cases.
intro: Comparative analysis asks how different systems deal with a common problem. The trap is to compare labels. Two countries may each have a "court" or an "election" that works quite differently. The useful questions are what an institution does, who can use it and whether its decisions are obeyed.
  show: table Question | Why it matters ;; what does it do? | the same name may hide different functions ;; who can use it? | access may be cheap or prohibitive ;; are its decisions obeyed? | formal power may not be real power
demo: Two invented systems both allow "review of official decisions". In one, an application is free, is decided within a month and its rulings are enforced. In the other, the fee equals a month's wages, cases take years and rulings are often ignored. The same formal label conceals very different chances of challenging those in power.
  show: table System | Cost of applying | Delay | Rulings obeyed? ;; first | free | one month | yes ;; second | a month's wages | several years | often not
C: Both systems have "review of official decisions". Are they equivalent?
  left: text Free to apply, decided within a month, rulings enforced.
  right: text A fee of a month's wages, years of delay, rulings often ignored.
  + No: the label is the same, but the practical chance of a remedy is not :: Compare function and access.
  - Yes: both have the institution :: [label-equivalence] Having something called "review" is not the same as being able to use it.
  - They cannot be compared :: [incommensurable] They can be compared on cost, delay and compliance.
  hint: Could an ordinary person obtain a remedy in each?
  why: Institutions are compared by what they deliver in practice, not by what they are called.
G: A formal rule, or the practice?
  bin Formal rule: The constitution guarantees an independent judiciary. | The statute lets any citizen appeal.
  bin Practice: Judges who rule against ministers are moved to distant posts. | Appeals take six years on average. | One appeal in fifty is ever heard.
  hint: Is it what the text says, or what actually happens?
  why: Written design and real practice can diverge. A comparison needs evidence of both.
M: Match each function of a constitution to what it does.
  pairs: Allocating authority => Says who may decide what | Limiting power => Says what the government may not do | Protecting rights => Lists guarantees for individuals | Providing for amendment => Says how the document itself may be changed
  hint: Which function concerns changing the constitution?
  why: Constitutions perform several distinct functions, and systems differ in how strongly they perform each.
P: "Country A has a written constitution and is stable. Country B has none and is unstable. So written constitutions cause stability." What is wrong?
  + Two countries differ in countless ways, and a comparison of two cannot isolate one cause :: Too few cases, and too many differences.
  - Nothing: the pattern is clear :: [small-comparison-as-proof] One pair of cases cannot separate the constitution from everything else.
  - Constitutions have no effect on anything :: [overcorrection] They may matter. This comparison cannot show it.
  hint: In how many other ways might A and B differ?
  why: Small comparisons generate hypotheses. A causal claim needs many cases, or carefully matched ones.
>P: A researcher compares the offices that handle complaints against officials in two invented states, by reading their founding statutes alone, and concludes that they are equally effective. What is the criticism?
  + Statutes show formal powers; effectiveness needs evidence of practice: who can reach the office, what it decides and whether officials comply :: The formal rule is not the practice.
  - None: the statutes are identical :: [formal-rule-as-practice] Identical powers on paper can be used very differently.
  - Statutes are irrelevant :: [dismissed-formal-rules] They matter as the starting point. They are simply not sufficient.
  hint: What would show that an office actually changes anything?
  why: To compare effectiveness you must observe outcomes. The text of a statute describes what is permitted, not what happens.
done: Compare what institutions do, not what they are called. Take one institution in your country and ask who can really use it.

@ civics.4.policy-evaluation
goal: Design an evaluation of a policy that covers effectiveness, fairness and process, and criticise a claim that rests on a comparison of before with after.
intro: Evaluating a policy means asking what happened, and what would have happened without it. It also means asking who gained and who lost, whether the policy was carried out as intended, and whether the process was fair. No single measure answers all of these.
  show: table Question | Kind ;; did it achieve its aim? | effectiveness ;; who gained, and who lost? | equity ;; was it carried out fairly, and as planned? | process ;; what did each unit of result cost? | cost-effectiveness
demo: A library extends its opening hours, and visits rise by 20%. But in the same month a neighbouring library closed. Its former users may account for the rise. A comparison of before with after cannot separate the two. The evaluation should also ask whether shift workers gained access, and whether the staffing can be sustained.
  show: flow longer hours > more visits? ;; the library next door closes > more visits?
K: Did longer opening hours raise visits to the library? Turn over as few facts as you need to decide whether that has been established.
  clue Before and after: Visits rose by 20% after the hours were extended.
  clue Closure: A neighbouring library closed in the same month.
  clue Costs: Staffing costs rose by 12,000 a year.
  clue Survey: Visitors say that they like the longer hours.
  need: 2
  + Not established: the closure next door could account for the rise :: Two things changed at once.
  - Established: visits rose by 20% :: [before-after-as-causal] The rise coincides with another change.
  hint: Did anything else change at the same time?
  why: A comparison of before with after credits the policy with everything that changed. Here a second cause cannot be separated from it.
G: What kind of evaluation question is each?
  bin Effectiveness: Did visits rise because of the longer hours?
  bin Equity: Did shift workers and carers gain access? | Which neighbourhoods benefited?
  bin Process: Were residents consulted before the change? | Were the staff rotas agreed fairly?
  hint: Did it work, for whom, and was it done properly?
  why: A policy can succeed on one of these and fail on another. An evaluation should report each.
F: Calculate a cost-effectiveness ratio.
  frame: Longer hours cost 12,000 more a year and bring 4,000 more visits. The cost of each extra visit is __
  answer: 3 | three
  x 48000000 :: [multiplied] Divide the extra cost by the extra visits.
  x 0.33 :: [inverted-the-ratio] Divide the cost by the visits, not the visits by the cost.
  hint: 12,000 ÷ 4,000.
  why: 12,000 ÷ 4,000 = 3 for each extra visit. Whether that is worth paying is a judgement of value that the ratio cannot make.
P: Libraries are funded according to the number of visits recorded. One begins to count everyone who comes in to use the lavatory. What has gone wrong?
  + The measure is being raised without the goal being served :: An indicator used as a target invites gaming.
  - Nothing: the visits really did rise :: [indicator-as-goal] The count rose, and reading and learning did not.
  - Visits should never be counted :: [overcorrection] Counting is useful. Tying funding to one count is what distorts it.
  hint: What was the count supposed to tell us?
  why: When a measure becomes a target, people optimise the measure. Evaluations should watch for that, and use more than one indicator.
>P: A town gives free bus passes to everyone under eighteen, and their journeys rise by 15%. In the same year the price of fuel doubled. How should the claim "the passes raised bus use" be tested?
  + Against a group not given passes, such as adults in the same town or young people in a similar town :: That separates the passes from the price of fuel.
  - It needs no test: use rose after the passes were given :: [before-after-as-causal] Dearer fuel may have pushed everyone onto the buses.
  - It cannot be tested :: [gave-up] A sensible comparison group makes it testable.
  hint: Who faced the same fuel prices, and did not receive a pass?
  why: A comparison group that shared the other changes, but not the policy, shows what the policy added.
done: Did it work, for whom, by what process, and compared with what? Evaluate one local policy on all four.

@ civics.5.research-methods
goal: Design a study of law in practice that combines interpretation with sound empirical inference, and criticise its sampling and its measures.
intro: Sociolegal research studies how law works in people's lives. Doctrinal analysis interprets what the legal materials require. Empirical research finds out what actually happens. Combining the two requires an explicit link between a legal concept and whatever is measured.
  show: table Approach | Asks | Uses ;; doctrinal | what does the law require? | statutes, decisions, commentary ;; empirical | what happens in practice? | records, interviews, observation
demo: A study asks why people abandon an invented complaints process. Interviewing only those who completed it would miss exactly the people the study is about. A sample chosen deliberately across every stage can reveal why people drop out, and the office's records can show how many do, provided that the gaps in those records are understood.
  show: flow complaint begun > some abandon > some complete > who was interviewed?
G: A doctrinal question, or an empirical one?
  bin Doctrinal: What does the statute require of the complaints office? | How have tribunals interpreted "within a reasonable time"?
  bin Empirical: How many complainants abandon the process, and at which stage? | How do complainants describe their experience?
  hint: Is it about what the law says, or about what happens?
  why: The two kinds of question need different methods. Many research problems need both.
F: Use the office's records.
  frame: Of 400 complaints begun, 240 were completed. The share abandoned is __ %
  answer: 40
  x 60 :: [counted-the-completers] Sixty per cent completed. The question asks about those who did not.
  x 160 :: [count-as-share] One hundred and sixty is the number abandoned. Divide it by 400.
  hint: 400 − 240, as a share of 400.
  why: 160 ÷ 400 = 40%.
P: The office records a case as "resolved" whenever the file is closed, including when the complainant has given up. What is the problem with using that as a measure of success?
  + The measure does not capture the concept: a closed file is not a complaint put right :: A problem of validity.
  - There is none: "resolved" means resolved :: [label-as-measure] The label covers people who abandoned the process.
  - Administrative records can never be used :: [overcorrection] They can, once their definitions are understood.
  hint: What does "resolved" include?
  why: A measure must correspond to the concept being studied. Administrative categories are built for administration, not for research.
C: Two sources for the same study. What does each contribute?
  left: text Thirty interviews, chosen to cover every stage at which people drop out.
  right: text The office's records of all 400 complaints.
  + The interviews show why people abandon; the records show how many, and when: each needs the other :: Mechanism and pattern.
  - The records make the interviews unnecessary :: [numbers-over-meaning] Records cannot explain what the experience was like.
  - The interviews make the records unnecessary :: [meaning-over-numbers] Thirty people cannot show how common each path is.
  hint: Which source answers "why", and which answers "how many"?
  why: Qualitative and quantitative evidence answer different questions. Used together, each covers the other's blind spot.
P: The researcher once worked in the complaints office that is being studied. What does good practice require?
  + Saying so, and considering how it may shape access, the interviews and their interpretation :: Reflexivity.
  - Concealing it, so as to appear neutral :: [hidden-position] Concealment prevents readers from weighing its influence.
  - Giving up the study :: [overcorrection] Inside knowledge can help, if it is declared and examined.
  hint: What would a reader need to know in order to judge the findings?
  why: Researchers are part of what they study. Declaring one's position, and reflecting on it, is part of rigour.
>P: The office deletes the records of abandoned complaints after thirty days. The study asks why people abandon. What follows for its design?
  + The missing records are precisely the cases of interest, so the study must recruit people as they enter, and document what is missing :: The surviving records are not representative.
  - Nothing: analyse the records that remain :: [missing-as-random] What remains is mostly the completers.
  - The study is impossible :: [gave-up] It can be designed prospectively.
  hint: Which cases survive the deletion?
  why: Data that are missing because of the very thing being studied bias any analysis of what is left. The design has to capture the cases before they vanish.
done: What the law says, what happens, and who is missing from the data. Read one study of law in practice and find its sampling decision.

@ civics.5.governance-capstone
goal: Develop and defend a proposal for institutional reform that weighs principles against evidence, anticipates unintended effects and builds in review and correction.
intro: Reforming an institution raises two kinds of question. Normative questions ask what is owed to people: fairness, a hearing, a reason. Empirical questions ask what the reform will actually do, given incentives and resources. A good proposal answers both, compares alternatives and plans for being wrong.
  show: flow compare the alternatives > run a pilot > measure the errors and who bears them > provide a route of challenge > review and correct
demo: An invented review office proposes automated triage to shorten its delays. Three options are compared: triage by staff, a limited pilot of the tool, and full introduction. The analysis asks how often urgent cases are wrongly put back, whether errors fall more on some applicants than on others, and how a person can challenge a decision.
  show: table Option | Speed | Risk ;; triage by staff | slow | familiar ;; a limited pilot | moderate | contained, and can be studied ;; full introduction | fast | unknown, and hard to reverse
G: A normative consideration, or an empirical one?
  bin Normative: Anyone affected is entitled to ask for a human decision. | Like cases should be treated alike.
  bin Empirical: How often does the tool put an urgent case in the wrong queue? | Do the errors differ between groups of applicants? | How long do cases wait under each option?
  hint: Is it about what people are owed, or about what will happen?
  why: Principles set the standards. Evidence shows whether a design meets them. A reform needs both.
O: Put the stages of a cautious reform in order.
  items: Compare the feasible alternatives | Run a limited pilot under staff oversight | Measure the errors, and who bears them | Provide a way to challenge a decision | Review at a fixed date, and correct or withdraw
  ends: First | Last
  hint: Evidence from a pilot comes before any wider use.
  why: Compare, pilot, measure, protect, review. Each stage limits the harm done if the idea proves wrong.
P: The office sets a target that every case be closed within thirty days. What unintended effect should the analysis anticipate?
  + Staff may close difficult cases quickly and badly in order to meet the target :: The measure displaces the goal.
  - None: a target only speeds things up :: [no-unintended-effects] People respond to what is measured.
  - The staff will ignore the target :: [ignored-incentives] Targets tied to assessment are seldom ignored.
  hint: Which cases are hardest to close within thirty days?
  why: Rules change incentives. A reform should ask how people will actually respond to it, and not only what it intends.
P: When the triage tool wrongly puts an urgent case at the back of the queue, who is most likely to bear the harm?
  + Applicants who are least able to chase, complain or get help :: Errors fall hardest on those with the fewest resources.
  - The office's managers :: [wrong-bearer] They bear little of the cost of a delayed case.
  - Everyone equally :: [equal-burden-assumed] Those who can press their case are overlooked less often.
  hint: Who notices and objects when a case is delayed?
  why: The costs of error are seldom spread evenly. Asking who bears them is central to judging whether a reform is fair.
S: Select every mechanism that allows a reform to be reviewed and corrected.
  + A right to ask for a decision to be reviewed by a person :: A route of challenge.
  + Publication of the rates of error :: Problems become visible.
  + An independent audit :: An examination by someone with no stake.
  + A fixed date for reassessment :: Review is built in.
  - An assurance that the tool is accurate :: [assurance-as-safeguard] A promise is not a check.
  - Secrecy about how cases are ranked :: [opacity] What cannot be seen cannot be challenged.
  hint: Which of these could reveal a problem and lead to a change?
  why: Correction requires that errors be visible, challengeable and independently examined, with review scheduled in advance.
>P: The office must choose between introducing the tool everywhere at once and a limited pilot. Which is the more defensible, and why?
  + The pilot: it can be reversed, it yields evidence about errors and access, and it keeps a human route open while that evidence is gathered :: Proportionate to the uncertainty.
  - Full introduction: it cuts the delays soonest :: [speed-over-evidence] It also spreads any error to every case before anyone can detect it.
  - Neither: automation should never be used :: [categorical-rejection] The question is how to find out whether it helps, with safeguards.
  hint: Under which option could a mistake be found and undone?
  why: When the effects are uncertain and the harms fall on vulnerable people, a reversible, monitored step is easier to defend than an irreversible one.
done: Principles and evidence, alternatives and safeguards, and a plan for being wrong. Draft a one-page proposal for improving a process you know.
